course: dictionary
lesson: 4601
title: Meet the heron
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A heron is a tall bird with long legs, a long neck and a long sharp beak. Its name is not hard to picture once you know what it looks like.
explain: «Herons stand very still at the edge of garden ponds to catch fish, so pond owners use nets or give the fish places to hide.» Still, edge, ponds and fish are the clues: a heron waits by water to catch fish.
explain: «A grey heron stands perfectly still in shallow water, then shoots out its S-shaped neck like a spring to grab a fish or frog.» The words still and spring show how a heron hunts: it waits, then strikes fast.
explain: «Cranes fly with necks stretched out straight, while herons fly with necks folded back in an S.» The word while shows a difference. You can tell a heron from a crane by how its neck looks in flight.
fact: A heron is a tall wading bird with long legs, a long neck and a long sharp beak.
fact: Herons hunt by waiting very still in shallow water and then striking fast to catch fish and frogs.
fact: Herons live near rivers, lakes and marshes in many countries.
fact: The grey heron and the great blue heron are both common herons.
fact: «A straw in water looks bent» because light changes direction as it passes from water into air, so a fish is not quite where it looks. Herons aim carefully because of this.
exercise: «Herons stand very still at the edge of garden ponds to catch fish.» What do herons do to catch fish? | Dive under the water and swim | Chase them fast across the pond | *Wait very still, then strike fast | Fly high above the trees
exercise: What is a heron? | A type of crane with a short beak | A small fish that lives in a pond | A kind of frog with long legs | *A tall bird with long legs, a long neck and a long sharp beak
exercise: «A grey heron stands perfectly still in shallow water.» Where does the heron stand? | In deep, dark caves | On top of a tall tree | *In shallow water | In the sea waves
test: «Cranes fly with necks stretched out straight, while herons fly with necks folded back in an S.» How do herons fly differently from cranes? | *Herons fly with their necks folded back in an S | Herons fly with straight necks, like cranes | Herons fly with their legs tucked under them | Herons do not fly at all
test: Where do herons live? | Only on the tops of mountains | Only in deep caves | In hot deserts with no water | *Near rivers, lakes and marshes
exam: Which of these is a heron? | A fish that swims in a pond | *A grey heron standing in shallow water | A grey bird that lives in a cage | A crane flying with its neck stretched out straight
